CODE EXPLAINED
1. Import libraries
   pandas handles tabular data; matplotlib draws graphs; load_iris loads the dataset; KMeans implements clustering.

2. df = load_iris(as_frame=True).frame
   Loads the Iris dataset as a pandas DataFrame. It contains four flower measurements and a target column.

3. X = df.iloc[:, 0:4]
   Selects the first four columns: sepal length, sepal width, petal length and petal width. The target column is not used for clustering.

4. wcss = [...]
   Calculates Within-Cluster Sum of Squares (WCSS) for cluster counts from 1 to 10.

5. range(1, 11)
   Generates the values 1, 2, ..., 10.

6. KMeans(n_clusters=k, n_init=10, random_state=42).fit(X).inertia_
   Creates and fits K-Means for each k. inertia_ gives WCSS, the sum of squared distances between samples and their assigned cluster centres. n_init=10 tries 10 initializations; random_state=42 makes initialization reproducible.

7. plt.plot(range(1, 11), wcss, marker='o')
   Plots number of clusters against WCSS. A bend or elbow can suggest a suitable k.

8. plt.title(), plt.xlabel(), plt.ylabel(), plt.show()
   Adds graph title and axis labels, then displays the Elbow graph.

9. df["Cluster"] = KMeans(n_clusters=3, n_init=10, random_state=42).fit_predict(X)
   Fits K-Means with three clusters and stores each sample's assigned cluster number in a new Cluster column.

10. plt.scatter(X.iloc[:, 0], X.iloc[:, 1], c=df.Cluster, cmap="viridis")
    Plots sepal length against sepal width. Point colours indicate assigned cluster numbers.

11. plt.xlabel(), plt.ylabel(), plt.title(), plt.show()
    Labels and displays the cluster visualization.

